p.src:
Two benchmark models have already appeared: $a(t)\propto t^{2/3}
\qquad\text{(matter domination)},$ and $a(t)\propto t^{1/2}
\qquad\text{(radiation domination)}.$ Neither law is exact for all times. Radiation gives the useful early-time approximation and matter the later intermediate one; vacuum energy eventually changes the late-time behavior again. The immediate question is not how observations reveal these eras, but what distinguishes the corresponding right-hand sides of Friedmann’s equation.

